//
//  Secrets.example.xcconfig
//  Anchor
//
//  Template. Copy, then fill in:
//
//      cp Config/Secrets.example.xcconfig Config/Secrets.xcconfig
//
//  Secrets.xcconfig is gitignored. This template is not.
//
//  ── Why the Google secret is required at all ────────────────────────────────
//
//  Google issues a client_secret for a "Desktop app" OAuth client and REQUIRES
//  it on the token exchange. Probed directly against Google on 2026-08-24:
//
//      POST https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token  (client_id + code + PKCE verifier,
//                                                 no client_secret)
//      -> {"error":"invalid_request","error_description":"client_secret is missing."}
//
//  PKCE alone does not authenticate this client type, however public the flow
//  looks in the browser. With no secret, every teacher consents in Google's UI
//  and only THEN fails — the worst possible place to meet an error, and the
//  exact failure PROSPECTS-era notes predicted but never tested.
//
//  ── Why it is not in OAuthClientDefaults.swift ──────────────────────────────
//
//  The repository is PUBLIC (github.com/rishabreddy0809/Anchor_MacOS). GitHub
//  secret scanning flags Google OAuth secrets and Google may auto-revoke one
//  that is pushed. The .gitignore header puts it more plainly: a secret
//  committed once stays recoverable from any clone.
//
//  Google's own position is that an installed-app secret "is not treated as a
//  secret" — which is why it is fine inside the shipped binary, and still not
//  fine in a public git history. Those are different claims, and this file is
//  the difference between them.
//
//  Get it from: console.cloud.google.com -> project anchor-504419 ->
//  APIs & Services -> Credentials -> the "Anchor macOS" Desktop client.
//
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET = paste-the-desktop-client-secret-here
